## PAGE 2 - TECHNICAL ANCHORS

### The two scripted answers (Jaimyn literally named these)
**useState:** state lives on the component's **fiber node**, keyed by **call order** (→ rules of hooks: unconditional, same order). The value is **captured in a closure per render**; the setter doesn't mutate - it **schedules a re-render**. Setters **batch** in handlers; updates replace, not merge; derive-from-old needs the **functional form** (stale-closure guard). Bail-out via **Object.is** → treat state as immutable.
**Event loop:** single thread, one call stack. Async work goes to host APIs; completions **queue**. **Microtasks (promises, await-continuations) drain fully after each task; macrotasks (setTimeout, I/O) one per turn** → promise beats setTimeout(0). `await` = suspend, resume as microtask. Long sync work blocks everything.

### Python (the named drill territory)
- **GIL:** exists because CPython uses **reference counting** - one lock keeps counts safe. So threads = concurrency for I/O, **zero CPU parallelism** → multiprocessing for CPU-bound, asyncio for high-concurrency I/O. (PEP 703 free-threading is the active fix.)
- **GC:** refcounting primary (immediate reclaim at zero) **+ generational cycle collector** for reference cycles.
- **Generators:** function with `yield` → calling runs nothing; each `next()` runs to the next yield and pauses with state intact; falling off the end raises **StopIteration** - the protocol a for-loop consumes. Lazy, constant memory, composable pipelines.
- **Why generators matter for AI (the tie-together):** "AI systems are streaming pipelines over I/O-bound calls with data bigger than memory - generators are Python's primitive for all three, and **async/await is generators grown up**, which is why FastAPI and FastMCP are built on it." LLM SDKs hand you the token stream as an async generator.

### Language comparisons (your home turf - invite these)
- **GC three ways:** Python = refcount + cycle collector (prompt, cycles need help). Java = **generational** tracing (most objects die young; Eden → promote; G1/ZGC). Go = **concurrent tri-color mark-sweep, non-generational**, tuned for sub-ms pauses + escape analysis keeps values off the heap.
- **Why no GIL in Go/Java:** no refcounting - tracing collectors built for concurrent mutation (write barriers, safepoints). The GIL is a CPython implementation choice, not a language necessity.
- **Concurrency:** goroutines M:N (true parallelism, cheap); Java OS threads + virtual threads since 21 (goroutines, twenty years later); Python threads real but GIL-bound.
- **Why Python over Go (near-certain):** "The bottleneck in an LLM system is the **model call**, not the language - Go's edge buys little, while Python buys the whole AI ecosystem first-class, research-speed iteration on prompts and evals, and a shared language with data science. I'd reach for Go where its virtues bind - CPU-bound, high-throughput infrastructure. **Language per workload, not preference.**"

### Agentic system design (the big synthesis question) - say the shape first
> "Three parts. **Frame the problem** and decide whether it even needs an agent. Then the **architecture by layers**: interface, orchestration, tools, knowledge and memory, data. Then the **production layer, where the real engineering is: safety, evals, ops.**"
- **Layer 0 gate:** does it need an agent? Agent = LLM in a loop choosing actions - only when the path varies per request. "I default to the most constrained design that solves it; autonomy adds latency, cost, and failure modes." (Travel planner: yes. Baggage FAQ: RAG, one retrieval.)
- **Orchestration:** controllable **graph** (LangGraph-style state machine) over free-running loop wherever stakes are real - bounded, inspectable.
- **Tools:** deterministic, authoritative layer under the probabilistic model - typed, validated; the model never invents a fare, it calls a tool. Descriptions are part of the interface.
- **Knowledge:** the RAG-vs-live-tool split - slow-changing + citable (policy) = RAG; volatile + must-be-exact (fares, availability) = live tool. "Knowing which is which is most of the design."
- **Safety:** input (injection - agents ingest untrusted tool/retrieved content), output (groundedness), **action** (the big one: state-changing tools need confirmation, idempotency, scoping - booking is the line). "The model is never the last line of defence on money or data."
- **Evals:** right tool + right args, task success rate, component + end-to-end, must-pass safety cases, **in CI** - "changing a tool description changes behaviour; without evals in CI you ship blind. Evals are TDD for AI."

### Quality scoring quick recall (if he drills evals)
**RAG triad:** context relevance → groundedness → answer relevance. **Cosine ≠ relevance** (retriever's internal score, not a quality metric). Recall@k (is it in top k) / **MRR** (1/rank of first hit) / **NDCG** (graded, whole ranking). **Faithfulness** = claims decomposed and checked vs context (the hallucination metric). **Perplexity ≠ correctness** (confident hallucinations score low). LLM-judge 1-5: validate vs human labels (verbosity/position/self-preference biases). **Score stages separately** - one end-to-end number hides where it broke.

### Cost + MCP cards
- **Costs /1M tokens:** Fable 5 $10/$50 · Opus 4.8 $5/$25 · Sonnet 5 $3/$15 · Haiku 4.5 $1/$5. Levers: **model routing** (biggest, 10x spread; judge on a cheaper model), cache reads ~0.1x, batch 50%, outputs cost ~5x inputs.
- **MCP:** a ChatGPT App **is a remote MCP server** - streamable HTTP + OAuth; stdio for local. Tools = typed functions, schema from type hints, description drives selection. Tool results return **complete**; "streaming" = transport + progress notifications; you consume the LLM's token stream server-side. **You built a FastMCP server this week - true, say it.**

